Digital Signature Service – Integration Guide
1. Overview

The Digital Signature Service provides a secure, banking-grade signing and verification mechanism for applications that require:

Transaction integrity
Non-repudiation
Two-factor authentication (2FA) for user verification

This service allows client applications to:

Initiate a signing request
Authenticate users with password + token or token-only 2FA
Sign payloads securely using server-side HSM-backed private keys
Validate signatures
Track the status of each signing request (INIT → SIGNED → USED)

The service ensures high security by keeping private keys on HSMs and never exposing them to clients.

2. Key Features
Initialization
Generates requestId and nonce per signing session.
Associates request with a user session.
2FA / Credential Verification
Supports password + token or token-only authentication.
Ensures that only authorized users can sign payloads.
Server-Side Signing
Payload hash includes a nonce to prevent replay attacks.
Signatures are generated using private keys in an HSM / Key Service.
Optionally, signature can be returned to client if needed.
Validation
Verifies payloads against stored signatures.
Updates status to prevent replay of signed requests.
Audit & Security
Logs requestId, user ID, IP, timestamp, and status transitions.
Handles errors such as expired nonce, failed signature, invalid session.
3. How Client Applications Can Use This Service
Step 1 – Initialize Signature Request
Call POST /api/dsig/init with sessionId.
Receive requestId and nonce.
Render digital signature UI in the client application using the nonce.
Step 2 – Authenticate User
Call POST /api/dsig/verify with user credentials (password + token or token-only) and requestId.
Receive status VERIFIED if authentication succeeds.
Step 3 – Sign Payload
Call POST /api/dsig/sign with requestId and payload.
Server computes hash with nonce and generates signature in HSM.
Status updated to SIGNED.
Optional: client receives signature for downstream use.
Step 4 – Validate Signature
Call POST /api/dsig/validate with requestId and payload.
Server verifies payload against stored signature.
Status updated to USED.
Response indicates VALID or INVALID.
4. API Summary
Step	Endpoint	Request	Response	Notes
Init	/api/dsig/init	{ "sessionId": "string" }	{ "requestId": "string", "nonce": "string", "status": "INIT" }	Generates requestId & nonce
Verify	/api/dsig/verify	{ "requestId": "string", "credentials": {...} }	{ "status": "VERIFIED" / "FAILED" }	Authenticate user via 2FA
Sign	/api/dsig/sign	{ "requestId": "string", "payload": "string" }	{ "status": "SIGNED", "signature": "optional" }	Payload signed server-side
Validate	/api/dsig/validate	{ "requestId": "string", "payload": "string" }	{ "status": "VALID" / "INVALID" }	Verify signed payload
5. Security Considerations
Private keys are never exposed to clients.
Nonce ensures replay protection.
Each request is bound to a specific session.
Audit logging supports regulatory compliance.
Error handling covers invalid sessions, signature failures, and expired nonces.
6. Integration Benefits for Client Applications
Secure Transaction Signing – Applications can trust that signed payloads are tamper-proof.
Regulatory Compliance – Audit logs and secure key handling meet banking and financial regulations.
Simplified Implementation – Clients only call APIs, without managing private keys or HSMs.
Flexible Integration – Optional signature return allows integration with downstream systems.
2FA Support – Enhances security for high-value transactions.
7. Recommended Client Flow
Application renders signature UI after initialization.
User enters credentials for 2FA verification.
Application calls sign API with payload.
Optional: retrieve signature if needed.
Before sending to downstream systems, optionally validate signature.
Update application status / logs based on validation response.

